%% D5 — Scale: what was measured, what the arithmetic gives,
%% where the load is spread, and what breaks first.
%% Show this while narrating the load-test numbers.

flowchart TB

subgraph HARN["The harness — why these numbers mean anything"]
  direction TB
  H1["Locust · perf/ · 28 runs, CSV + HTML in the repo"]
  H2["forges a DISTINCT IP per request<br/>otherwise my own per-IP rate limiter<br/>becomes the bottleneck of my own test"]
  H3["AI paths excluded and disabled<br/>mixing LLM latency into a CRUD average<br/>produces a number that describes nothing"]
  H4["realistic think time: one click / 5–10s<br/>because the real question is<br/>'how many PEOPLE fit', not 'how many loops'"]
end

subgraph MEAS["MEASURED — I have the report"]
  direction TB
  M1["250 users → 33 req/s · p95 26ms · 0% · idle"]
  M2["500 users → 65 req/s · p95 46ms · 0% · comfortable"]
  M3["750 users → 99 req/s · p95 545ms · 0% ← RECOMMENDED CEILING"]
  M4["1000 users → 120 req/s · p95 1.1s · 0.2% ← first errors"]
  M5["1500 users → 89% failures ← the wall<br/>p95 DROPS to 290ms here: connections rejected<br/>instantly, not served slowly — kernel resetting<br/>a full accept backlog, nothing reaches my code"]
  M6["NEVER DIED: Restarts=0 · OOMKilled=false<br/>224MB of 1GB · healthy in EVERY run,<br/>including the 89%-failure one<br/>it degrades and refuses. it does not fall over."]
  M1 --> M2 --> M3 --> M4 --> M5
  M5 --- M6
end

subgraph PROOF["THE PROOF IT SCALES — same box, same 4 CPUs, same load"]
  direction TB
  W1["1 worker  → 3,911 reqs · 66 req/s · p95 3200ms · 0 fail"]
  W4["4 workers → 8,279 reqs · 140 req/s · p95 1200ms · 0 fail"]
  WD["2.11x throughput · p95 2.67x BETTER<br/>only change: '--workers 4' · zero app code<br/><br/>req/s PER WORKER is nearly identical in both —<br/>4 cores to ONE uvicorn process does nothing,<br/>because one process uses one core"]
  W1 --> W4 --> WD
end

subgraph END["ENDURANCE — throughput without it is worthless"]
  direction TB
  E1["112,549 reqs · 9 min · 208 req/s steady<br/>ZERO failures · p95 75ms<br/>memory 510→570MB in minute 1 (workers warming),<br/>then FLAT 568–571MB for 8 minutes<br/>no leak · no restart · no OOM"]
  E2["SOAK: 1,263,978 reqs · 3h34m<br/>300 concurrent · 0.26% failures"]
  E3["2,455,264 requests across all 28 runs"]
  E1 --> E2 --> E3
end

COEF["THE COEFFICIENT — this is the only measured conversion<br/><br/>750 users produced 99 req/s → 0.13 req/s PER USER<br/><br/>everything below is multiplication, and I say so out loud"]

subgraph CALC["CALCULATED — arithmetic, not measurement"]
  direction TB
  C1["80,000 users × 0.13 ≈ 10,600 req/s<br/>÷180 req/s per worker ≈ 60 cores<br/>+40% headroom ≈ 84 cores ≈ 21 containers of 4 vCPU"]
  C2["1,000,000 users × 0.13 ≈ 130,000 req/s<br/>÷180 ≈ 722 cores<br/>+40% headroom ≈ 1,010 cores ≈ 250 containers"]
  C3["the 40% is not padding: bursts, rolling deploys,<br/>and one whole availability zone going away"]
  C1 --> C2 --> C3
end

subgraph SPREAD["WHERE LOAD IS SPREAD — four levels, four problems"]
  direction TB
  S1["1 · EDGE — Traefik<br/>loadbalancer.server.port=8000<br/>adding a replica = adding a container"]
  S2["2 · IN-CONTAINER — UVICORN_WORKERS<br/>the 2.11x multiplier · one env var<br/>the cheapest lever in the system"]
  S3["3 · DATABASE — PgBouncer, transaction mode<br/>makes NOTHING faster today<br/>preventive: it exists so 1 and 2 can grow"]
  S4["4 · BACKGROUND — Celery fan-out<br/>a 40-item plan = 40 independent tasks<br/>across every worker"]
  S5["5 · (opposite direction) LLM semaphore = 5<br/>limiting my OWN outbound calls,<br/>because blowing the provider quota<br/>is DDoSing yourself"]
end

subgraph BREAK["WHAT BREAKS FIRST — in order. the API is the EASY part."]
  direction TB
  B0["WHY the API is easy: it is STATELESS.<br/>session is a JWT cookie, not server memory →<br/>any replica serves any user →<br/>throughput is bought with copies.<br/>The hard part is what those copies SHARE."]
  B1["1 · POSTGRES CONNECTIONS — breaks TODAY<br/>max_connections=100 · each API process opens up to 30<br/>→ THREE processes before refusal, long before CPU<br/>60 workers would want 1,800 raw connections<br/>FIX: PgBouncer (already in the stack), app pool → ~5"]
  B2["2 · POSTGRES READ CAPACITY<br/>the mix is read-heavy: /subjects /plans /auth/me<br/>FIX: read replicas (GET→replica, write→primary) +<br/>short-TTL Redis cache. The Service pattern makes this<br/>ONE change in ONE place instead of every router."]
  B3["3 · REDIS CONTENTION<br/>rate limit + cache + broker on one instance<br/>FIX: split the roles. >100k ops/s per instance,<br/>so this is tuning, not redesign."]
  B4["4 · ARGON2 UNDER A LOGIN STORM<br/>expensive on CPU BY DESIGN — that is the point.<br/>Invisible in steady state (15-min cookie, rare logins).<br/>But 80k people at 9am is a spike of its own shape.<br/>FIX: auth-dedicated replica pool + tune the cost<br/>parameter against measurement.<br/>The harness has AUTH_WEIGHT to produce it on purpose."]
  B0 --> B1 --> B2 --> B3 --> B4
end

FLAW["MY OWN SYSTEM'S FLAW — I present it before you find it<br/><br/>an exhausted DB pool returns 500 today. It should be<br/>503 + Retry-After, because it is BACKPRESSURE, not an error.<br/>Consequence 1: client and LB never learn to back off.<br/>Consequence 2 (worse): on the dashboard those 500s are<br/>indistinguishable from a real bug — I polluted my own<br/>error signal with load.<br/>Written in perf/RESULTS.md. One status code, one handler."]

VERD["THE HONEST VERDICT<br/><br/>Can it reach 1M? YES — stateless API + managed Postgres/Redis.<br/>10k req/s is a medium deployment, not an exotic one.<br/>By adding replicas alone? NO — connections and read capacity<br/>break first, and those two ARE the engineering project.<br/>Rewrite? NO — infrastructure + 3 small code changes:<br/>a pool setting, one cache, one status code.<br/><br/>I did NOT test 1M. Nobody does from one laptop.<br/>I tested the COEFFICIENT, and found it linear."]

HARN ==> MEAS
MEAS ==> PROOF
PROOF ==> END
END ==> COEF
COEF ==> CALC
CALC ==> SPREAD
SPREAD ==> BREAK
BREAK --- FLAW
BREAK ==> VERD

